/* ======================= PERMISSIONS ======================= */
lesson('chmod', {
  n: 'chmod', s: 'Changes who may read, write or execute a file.',
  an: 'Changing the locks on a door: one key for the owner, one for the family (group), one for visitors (others) — each key can allow entering, writing on the walls, or running the machines inside.',
  nm: '<b>ch</b>ange <b>mod</b>e (the “mode” = permission bits).',
  sx: 'chmod [OPTIONS] MODE FILE...', p: [['chmod', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-R recursive, -v verbose.', false], ['MODE', 'arg', 'Symbolic (u+x, go-w, a=r) or octal (755, 644).', true], ['FILE...', 'arg', 'Files/directories to change.', true]],
  ex1: 'chmod u+x script.sh',
  sc: [['newfstatat', 'reads the current mode (needed for symbolic changes like u+x)', 'newfstatat(AT_FDCWD, "script.sh", {st_mode=S_IFREG|0644, …}, 0) = 0'], ['fchmodat', 'writes the new mode into the inode — only the owner or root may do this', 'fchmodat(AT_FDCWD, "script.sh", 0744) = 0']],
  ch: 'Only the permission bits in the inode (and its ctime). File content is untouched.', out: 'Nothing (with -v / -c: a line describing the change).', ec: '0 = OK, 1 = e.g. “Operation not permitted” (not the owner) or no such file.',
  f: [['-R', '--recursive', 'Apply to a directory and everything inside.', 'chmod -R g+w shared/', true], ['-v', '--verbose', 'Report every file processed.', 'chmod -v 600 f', true], ['-c', '--changes', 'Report only files that actually changed.', 'chmod -c 644 *', false], ['u g o a', '', 'WHO: user (owner), group, others, all.', 'chmod g+w f', true], ['+ - =', '', 'Add, remove, or set exactly.', 'chmod o-rwx f', true], ['r w x', '', 'WHICH: read (4), write (2), execute (1).', 'chmod +x f', true], ['X', '', 'x only for directories (and files already executable) — great with -R.', 'chmod -R a+X dir', false], ['--reference', '', 'Copy the mode of another file.', 'chmod --reference=a b', false]],
  e: [['chmod u+x script.sh', '', 'Let the owner execute the script.'], ['chmod 755 script.sh', '', 'rwxr-xr-x: owner all, others read+execute.'], ['chmod 600 notes.txt', '', 'rw-------: private file.'], ['chmod go-w data.csv', '', 'Remove write for group and others.'], ['chmod -v a=r fruits.txt', "mode of 'fruits.txt' changed from 0644 (rw-r--r--) to 0444 (r--r--r--)", 'Read-only for everyone.'], ['chmod -R u=rwX,go=rX projects', '', 'Safe recursive fix: dirs enterable, files not made executable.']],
  u: `<p><b>Three sets × three bits</b> (shown by <code>ls -l</code>): <code>-rwxr-xr--</code> = owner rwx, group r-x, others r--.</p><table><tr><th></th><th>File</th><th>Directory</th></tr><tr><td>r</td><td>read content</td><td>list names (ls)</td></tr><tr><td>w</td><td>change content</td><td>create / delete / rename entries</td></tr><tr><td>x</td><td>run as a program</td><td>enter it (cd) and reach files inside</td></tr></table><p><b>Octal:</b> r = 4, w = 2, x = 1 — add them per set. rwx = 7, rw- = 6, r-x = 5, r-- = 4. So 755 = rwxr-xr-x, 644 = rw-r--r--, 600 = rw-------. Try the calculator:</p><div data-calc="754"></div><p>The kernel checks only ONE set: if you're the owner, the owner bits apply (even if “others” would allow more); else if you're in the group, the group bits; else others. root bypasses r/w checks.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>The mode is the low 12 bits of <code>inode->i_mode</code> (plus 4 type bits). <code>chmod(2)</code> → <code>notify_change()</code> → filesystem setattr; permission checks happen in <code>generic_permission()</code> via <code>inode_permission()</code> on every open/exec/lookup. If the caller isn't in the file's group, the kernel silently clears setgid. Inspect: <code>stat -c '%a %A %n' file</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['chmod 777 file', 'Everyone on the system can modify (or replace) it — a classic security hole.', 'chmod 755 dir   /   chmod 644 file'], ['chmod -R 755 ~/project', 'Makes every file executable, including documents.', 'chmod -R u=rwX,go=rX ~/project'], ['chmod +x script.sh; script.sh', '“command not found”: the current directory isn\'t in $PATH.', './script.sh'], ['chmod 644 mydir', 'Removes x from the directory — now nobody can cd into it or open files inside.', 'chmod 755 mydir']],
  w: '<b>chmod -R</b> changes thousands of files at once and there is no undo; <b>chmod 777</b> lets any user change or replace the file (e.g. a script others run). Prefer the minimum: 644 for files, 755 for directories and programs, 600/700 for private data. Never chmod system directories like /etc or /usr recursively.',
  r: [['chown', 'chmod sets WHAT is allowed; chown sets WHO the owner/group are.'], ['umask', 'Controls the permissions NEW files start with.'], ['chmod-octal', 'Deep dive: octal math and special bits.'], ['ls', 'ls -l shows the bits.']],
  cs: ['<code>chmod u+x f</code> owner may execute', '<code>chmod 644 f</code> file · <code>755</code> dir/program', 'r=4 w=2 x=1, per owner/group/others', 'Directories need x to be entered', 'Avoid <code>777</code>; use <code>-R</code> carefully'],
  q: [['What does chmod 640 set?', ['rw-r-----', 'rwxr--r--', 'rw-rw----', 'r--r-----'], 0, '6 = rw-, 4 = r--, 0 = ---.'], ['Which permission lets you cd into a directory?', ['r', 'w', 'x', 'none'], 2, 'x on a directory means "search/enter".'], ['Who may run chmod on a file?', ['Anyone with r', 'Anyone with w', 'The owner or root', 'Only root'], 2, 'Even write permission isn\'t enough — you must own it.']],
  c: { t: 'Make <code>script.sh</code> executable for its owner, then run it as <code>./script.sh</code>.', try: 'chmod u+x script.sh', steps: [['script.sh has the owner x bit', ctx => { const r = HP('script.sh'); return r.n && (r.n.mode & 0o100); }], ['Run ./script.sh successfully', ctx => /\.\/script\.sh/.test(ctx.line) && ctx.code === 0]] },
});
lesson('chown', {
  n: 'chown', s: 'Changes the owner (and optionally the group) of files.',
  an: 'Transferring the deed of a house to a new owner. Only the land registry (root) can do it — you can\'t just give your house to someone, and you certainly can\'t take theirs.',
  nm: '<b>ch</b>ange <b>own</b>er.',
  sx: 'chown [OPTIONS] [OWNER][:GROUP] FILE...', p: [['chown', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-R recursive, -v verbose.', false], ['[OWNER][:GROUP]', 'arg', 'New owner, new group, or both: alice, alice:developers, :developers.', true], ['FILE...', 'arg', 'Targets.', true]],
  ex1: 'sudo chown alice notes.txt',
  sc: [['newfstatat', 'looks up the file', 'newfstatat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", …) = 0'], ['(user lookup)', 'translates the name alice → UID 1001 by reading /etc/passwd', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/passwd", O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC) = 3'], ['fchownat', 'sets the inode\'s UID/GID (requires root / CAP_CHOWN)', 'fchownat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", 1001, -1, 0) = 0']],
  ch: 'The UID/GID fields of the inode. For regular files the kernel also clears setuid/setgid bits for safety.', out: 'Nothing (with -v: <code>changed ownership of …</code>).', ec: '0 = OK, 1 = “Operation not permitted”, invalid user, etc.',
  f: [['-R', '--recursive', 'Apply to a whole directory tree.', 'sudo chown -R www-data /var/www', true], ['-v', '--verbose', 'Report each file.', 'sudo chown -v alice f', true], ['-h', '--no-dereference', 'Change a symlink itself, not its target.', 'sudo chown -h alice link', false], ['--reference', '', 'Copy owner/group from another file.', 'chown --reference=a b', false], ['user:', '', 'Owner AND their login group.', 'sudo chown alice: f', false]],
  e: [['chown alice notes.txt', 'chown: changing ownership of \'notes.txt\': Operation not permitted', 'Normal users can\'t give files away.'], ['sudo chown alice notes.txt', '', 'Root can.'], ['sudo chown alice:developers data.csv', '', 'Owner and group in one go.'], ['chgrp developers fruits.txt', '', 'Owners may change the group to one they belong to.'], ['sudo chown -R user:user backup', '', 'Fix ownership of a whole tree.'], ['ls -l notes.txt', '-rw-r--r-- 1 alice user 104 …', 'Check the result.']],
  u: `<p>Each inode stores a numeric <b>UID</b> (owner) and <b>GID</b> (group). Names come from <code>/etc/passwd</code> and <code>/etc/group</code>; <code>ls -n</code> shows the raw numbers.</p><p>Only root may change the owner: otherwise users could hand files to others to escape disk quotas, or create files that appear to belong to someone else. A file's owner may change its group, but only to a group they are a member of (that's what <code>chgrp</code> does).</p>`,
  adv: `<p><code>chown(2)</code> requires <code>CAP_CHOWN</code>. When ownership changes, the kernel clears S_ISUID and (if group-executable) S_ISGID in <code>setattr_prepare()</code>, to prevent a setuid binary from silently becoming setuid to a new user. On NFS with root_squash, even root's chown may fail.</p>`,
  mi: [['chown alice file (as normal user)', '“Operation not permitted”.', 'sudo chown alice file'], ['sudo chown -R user /', 'Breaks the whole system (sudo, ssh and many services check ownership).', 'Only chown your own data directories'], ['chown user.group file', 'Old dot syntax — fails if names contain dots.', 'chown user:group file']],
  w: 'Running <b>chown -R</b> on the wrong directory (especially with sudo on system paths like /etc, /usr, /var) can make the system unbootable or break sudo/ssh. Double-check the path; never run it on /.',
  r: [['chmod', 'chown changes WHO; chmod changes WHAT they may do.'], ['groups', 'See which groups you can chgrp to.'], ['sudo', 'chown of other users\' files needs root.']],
  cs: ['<code>sudo chown alice f</code> new owner', '<code>sudo chown alice:dev f</code> owner + group', '<code>chgrp dev f</code> group only', '<code>-R</code> recursive — be careful', 'Only root can change the owner'],
  q: [['Why can\'t normal users give their files to someone else?', ['It\'s a bug', 'It would allow quota evasion and impersonation', 'Files have no owner', 'They can'], 1, 'Only root (CAP_CHOWN) may change ownership.'], ['Which sets owner alice and group developers?', ['chown alice-developers f', 'chown alice:developers f', 'chown developers:alice f', 'chown -g developers alice f'], 1, 'OWNER:GROUP.'], ['Where are owner names stored?', ['In the inode', 'In /etc/passwd (the inode stores numbers)', 'In the file content', 'In ~/.owner'], 1, 'Inodes hold numeric UIDs; names come from /etc/passwd.']],
  c: { t: 'Use sudo to make <b>alice</b> the owner of <code>fruits.txt</code>.', try: 'sudo chown alice fruits.txt', chk: ctx => { const r = HP('fruits.txt'); return r.n && r.n.uid === 1001; } },
});
lesson('whoami', {
  n: 'whoami', s: 'Prints the name of the user you are currently acting as.',
  an: 'Checking the name badge you are wearing right now — useful after you\'ve borrowed someone else\'s badge (sudo, su).',
  nm: '“<b>who am i</b>”.',
  sx: 'whoami', p: [['whoami', 'cmd', 'Takes no arguments.', true]],
  ex1: 'whoami',
  sc: [['geteuid', 'asks the kernel for the effective user ID of this process', 'geteuid() = 1000'], ['openat /etc/passwd', 'translates the number into a name', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/passwd", O_RDONLY|O_CLOEXEC) = 3'], ['write', 'prints the name', 'write(1, "user\\n", 5) = 5']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'One line with a user name, e.g. <code>user</code> or <code>root</code>.', ec: '0.',
  f: [['--help', '', 'Show usage (whoami has no other options; use id for more detail).', 'whoami --help', true]],
  e: [['whoami', 'user', 'Your normal identity.'], ['sudo whoami', 'root', 'Under sudo the command runs as root.'], ['id', 'uid=1000(user) gid=1000(user) groups=…', 'Full identity: numbers and all groups.'], ['id -un', 'user', 'Same as whoami.'], ['echo $USER', 'user', 'The login name variable (doesn\'t change with sudo -u in all cases).']],
  u: `<p>The kernel identifies users only by number. Each process has a <b>real UID</b> (who started it) and an <b>effective UID</b> (whose permissions it uses). whoami prints the effective one. <code>sudo</code> and setuid programs make these differ.</p><p><code>id</code> shows the full picture: UID, primary GID and supplementary groups — the groups decide which “group” permission bits apply to you.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Credentials live in <code>task_struct->cred</code> (uid, euid, suid, fsuid, gid…, group_info, capabilities). <code>/proc/self/status</code> lines Uid:/Gid: show real, effective, saved and filesystem IDs. whoami uses <code>geteuid()</code> + <code>getpwuid()</code> (NSS: /etc/passwd, LDAP, systemd-userdb…).</p>`,
  mi: [['who am i', 'Different command (who with args): shows your login session, may print nothing in some terminals.', 'whoami'], ['Trusting $USER inside sudo scripts', '$USER may still say your name.', 'id -un   # or whoami'], ['whoami alice', '“extra operand”.', 'id alice']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['sudo', 'Changes who you act as for one command.'], ['groups', 'Which groups you are in.'], ['su', 'Switch user for a whole shell.']],
  cs: ['<code>whoami</code> → effective user name', '<code>sudo whoami</code> → root', '<code>id</code> → UID, GID, groups', 'Kernel uses numbers (UID), names are labels', 'root = UID 0'],
  q: [['What does sudo whoami print?', ['your name', 'root', 'sudo', 'nothing'], 1, 'The command runs as root.'], ['What does the kernel use to identify users?', ['Names', 'Email', 'Numeric UIDs', 'Passwords'], 2, 'Names are looked up in /etc/passwd.'], ['Which shows all your groups too?', ['whoami -a', 'id', 'who', 'pwd'], 1, 'id prints uid, gid and groups.']],
  c: { t: 'Check who you are, then check who you are <b>under sudo</b>.', try: 'whoami', steps: [['Run whoami as yourself', ctx => okRun(ctx, 'whoami') && !ran(ctx, 'sudo') && outOf(ctx).trim() === 'user'], ['Run sudo whoami (prints root)', ctx => ran(ctx, 'sudo') && outOf(ctx).includes('root')]] },
});
lesson('sudo', {
  n: 'sudo', s: 'Runs a single command with root (administrator) privileges — if you are allowed to.',
  an: 'Showing your staff badge to a security guard to get into the server room for one task. The guard checks a list (sudoers), notes your visit in a logbook, and lets you in — only for that task.',
  nm: '<b>su</b>bstitute user <b>do</b> (originally “superuser do”).',
  sx: 'sudo [OPTIONS] COMMAND [ARGS]...', p: [['sudo', 'cmd', 'A setuid-root program.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-u USER run as someone else, -i root login shell, -l list your rights.', false], ['COMMAND [ARGS]...', 'arg', 'The command to run with elevated rights.', true]],
  ex1: 'sudo cat /etc/shadow',
  sc: [['execve (setuid)', '/usr/bin/sudo is owned by root with the s bit, so it STARTS with effective UID 0', 'execve("/usr/bin/sudo", ["sudo", "cat", "/etc/shadow"], …) = 0'], ['openat /etc/sudoers', 'reads the rules: is this user (or one of their groups) allowed?', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "/etc/sudoers", O_RDONLY) = 4'], ['PAM authentication', 'asks for YOUR password (cached ~15 min in /run/sudo/ts)', 'read(0, "********\\n", 255) = 9'], ['setresuid(0,0,0)', 'drops the remaining identity of the caller: real & effective UID become root', 'setresuid(0, 0, 0) = 0'], ['fork + execve', 'starts the real command as root', 'execve("/usr/bin/cat", ["cat", "/etc/shadow"], …) = 0']],
  ch: 'Whatever the command does — but now with root\'s power. sudo also writes a log line to /var/log/auth.log (or the journal).', out: 'The command\'s own output; plus the password prompt on first use; or “user is not in the sudoers file”.', ec: 'The command\'s exit code, or 1 if sudo itself refused.',
  f: [['-u USER', '--user', 'Run as another user instead of root.', 'sudo -u alice whoami', true], ['-i', '--login', 'Open a root login shell (exit to leave).', 'sudo -i', true], ['-l', '--list', 'Show what you are allowed to run.', 'sudo -l', true], ['-k', '--reset-timestamp', 'Forget the cached password.', 'sudo -k', false], ['-s', '--shell', 'Root shell without full login environment.', 'sudo -s', false], ['-E', '--preserve-env', 'Keep your environment variables.', 'sudo -E cmd', false], ['!!', '', '(bash) re-run the last command with sudo.', 'sudo !!', true]],
  e: [['cat /etc/shadow', 'cat: /etc/shadow: Permission denied', 'Password hashes are root-only.'], ['sudo cat /etc/shadow', 'root:*:19700:0:99999:7:::\n…', 'Same command with root rights.'], ['sudo whoami', 'root', 'Proof of identity change.'], ['sudo -u alice whoami', 'alice', 'Act as another (non-root) user.'], ['sudo -l', 'User user may run the following commands on linux:\n    (ALL : ALL) ALL', 'Your permissions.'], ['sudo apt update', '', 'Typical admin task.']],
  u: `<p><b>UID 0 = root</b>: the kernel skips most permission checks for it. That's why working as root all the time is dangerous — a typo can delete system files, and any malware you run can do anything.</p><p><b>/etc/sudoers</b> decides who may use sudo. On Ubuntu the line <code>%sudo ALL=(ALL:ALL) ALL</code> means “members of group sudo may run anything as anyone”. Edit it ONLY with <code>visudo</code>, which checks the syntax — a broken sudoers file can lock you out of sudo entirely.</p><p>Why your own password? It proves the person at the keyboard is you, without anyone needing to know root's password (on Ubuntu root has none).</p>`,
  adv: `<p>sudo is setuid root: the kernel sets euid=0 at exec because of the S_ISUID bit on the file. sudo then sanitises the environment (<code>env_reset</code>, <code>secure_path</code>), checks policy plugins (sudoers), authenticates via PAM, records a timestamp in <code>/run/sudo/ts/USER</code>, opens an I/O log if configured, and finally forks/execs the command with <code>setresuid(0,0,0)</code>, <code>setgroups()</code> and root's capabilities. Inspect rules with <code>sudo -l</code>; see logs with <code>journalctl _COMM=sudo</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['sudo cd /root', '“cd: command not found” — sudo runs programs, cd is a builtin.', 'sudo -i   (then cd /root)'], ['sudo echo "x" > /etc/file', '“Permission denied”: the REDIRECTION is done by your non-root shell before sudo runs.', 'echo "x" | sudo tee /etc/file'], ['sudo rm -rf $DIR/', 'If $DIR is empty this becomes rm -rf / — with full power.', 'rm -rf "\${DIR:?}"/ and think twice before sudo'], ['Editing /etc/sudoers with nano', 'One syntax error and sudo stops working.', 'sudo visudo']],
  w: '<b>Every sudo command runs with unlimited power.</b> There are no permission checks to save you from mistakes like <code>sudo rm -rf /</code>, <code>sudo chmod -R 777 /</code> or <code>sudo dd</code> to the wrong disk. Read the command twice, avoid copy-pasting sudo commands you don\'t understand, prefer <code>sudo -l</code> to check rights, and never run a whole session as root when one command will do.',
  r: [['su', 'su switches to another user for a whole shell and asks for THEIR password; sudo runs one command and asks for YOURS.'], ['whoami', 'Verify which user you are acting as.'], ['setuid', 'The special permission bit that makes sudo itself work.']],
  cs: ['<code>sudo cmd</code> run one command as root', 'Asks for YOUR password (cached ~15 min)', 'Allowed users: <code>/etc/sudoers</code> (edit with visudo)', '<code>sudo !!</code> redo the last command with sudo', 'Redirections run as YOU: use <code>| sudo tee file</code>'],
  q: [['Which password does sudo ask for?', ['root\'s', 'your own', 'the target user\'s', 'none'], 1, 'sudo authenticates the caller.'], ['Why does sudo echo hi > /etc/x fail?', ['echo is not allowed', 'The redirection is performed by your own (non-root) shell', '/etc is read-only', 'sudo needs -i'], 1, 'Use echo hi | sudo tee /etc/x.'], ['Where is it defined who may use sudo?', ['/etc/passwd', '/etc/sudoers', '/etc/shadow', '~/.sudo'], 1, 'Edit it with visudo.']],
  c: { t: 'Read the protected file <code>/etc/shadow</code> using sudo.', try: 'sudo cat /etc/shadow', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'sudo') && ctx.line.includes('/etc/shadow') && ctx.code === 0 },
});

/* ======================= PIPES & REDIRECTION ======================= */
lesson('pipe', {
  n: '| (pipe)', k: '|', s: 'Sends the output of one command directly into the input of the next.',
  an: 'An assembly line: each worker does one small job and hands the item straight to the next worker — nothing is stored in a warehouse (file) in between.',
  nm: 'The vertical bar <b>|</b> is called “pipe”: data flows through it like water.',
  sx: 'COMMAND1 | COMMAND2 [| COMMAND3]...', p: [['COMMAND1', 'cmd', 'Producer: its stdout goes into the pipe.', true], ['|', 'opt', 'The pipe operator (handled by the shell).', true], ['COMMAND2', 'cmd', 'Consumer: reads the pipe as its stdin. Can be chained further.', true]],
  ex1: 'grep apple fruits.txt | wc -l',
  sc: [['pipe2', 'the shell asks the kernel for a pipe: two file descriptors, a read end and a write end', 'pipe2([3, 4], O_CLOEXEC) = 0'], ['fork ×2', 'the shell starts BOTH commands at the same time', 'clone(…) = 2201; clone(…) = 2202'], ['dup2 (writer)', 'in the first child, stdout (fd 1) is replaced by the pipe\'s write end', 'dup2(4, 1) = 1'], ['dup2 (reader)', 'in the second child, stdin (fd 0) is replaced by the read end', 'dup2(3, 0) = 0'], ['write → read', 'grep writes matching lines into the kernel buffer; wc reads them', 'write(1, "apple\\napple\\napple\\n", 18) = 18'], ['wait4', 'the shell waits for both; the pipeline\'s exit code is the LAST command\'s', 'wait4(-1, [{WIFEXITED(s) && WEXITSTATUS(s) == 0}], 0, NULL) = 2202']],
  ch: 'Nothing on disk — data only passes through a memory buffer in the kernel.', out: 'Only the LAST command writes to your screen. Error messages (stderr) of every command still go to the screen; they do NOT go through the pipe.', ec: 'The exit code of the last command (use <code>set -o pipefail</code> to fail if any command fails; <code>\${PIPESTATUS[@]}</code> shows all).',
  f: [['|', '', 'Connect stdout → stdin.', 'ls | wc -l', true], ['|&', '', 'Pipe stdout AND stderr (= 2>&1 |).', 'make |& less', false], ['set -o pipefail', '', 'Pipeline fails if any command fails.', 'set -o pipefail', false], ['tee', '', 'Branch a pipe to a file too.', 'cmd | tee log | grep x', true]],
  e: [['ls | wc -l', '', 'Count files in the directory.'], ['grep apple fruits.txt | wc -l', '3', 'How many lines contain apple.'], ['sort fruits.txt | uniq -c | sort -rn', '      3 apple\n      2 banana…', 'Classic frequency count: 3 commands chained.'], ['cat data.csv | cut -d, -f2 | sort | uniq', 'dept\neng\nops\nsales', 'Unique departments.'], ['ps aux | grep python', '', 'Find processes by name.'], ['history | tail -5', '', 'Last five commands.']],
  u: `<p><b>File descriptors:</b> every process starts with 0 = stdin, 1 = stdout, 2 = stderr. A pipe is a pair of descriptors pointing at a <b>kernel buffer</b> (64 KiB by default). The shell connects the left command's fd 1 to the write end and the right command's fd 0 to the read end, then starts both.</p><p>All commands in a pipeline <b>run at the same time</b>. If the buffer is full, the writer pauses; if it's empty, the reader waits. When the writer exits, the reader sees end-of-file. When the reader exits early (e.g. head), the writer gets SIGPIPE.</p><p>The Unix philosophy: small tools that do one thing well, combined with pipes.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>pipe(2) creates a <code>pipe_inode_info</code> with a ring of 16 page-sized <code>pipe_buffer</code>s (resizable with <code>fcntl(F_SETPIPE_SZ)</code>, max /proc/sys/fs/pipe-max-size). Writes ≤ PIPE_BUF (4096 bytes) are atomic. Each command in a bash pipeline runs in its own subshell — that's why <code>echo x | read v</code> doesn't set v in your shell (use <code>shopt -s lastpipe</code> or process substitution). Look at a live pipe: <code>ls -l /proc/PID/fd</code> shows <code>pipe:[12345]</code>.</p>`,
  mi: [['ls | cd docs', 'cd ignores stdin and runs in a subshell — nothing happens.', 'cd docs'], ['grep x file | > out.txt', 'Syntax error / empty file: > needs a command.', 'grep x file > out.txt'], ['cmd 2>&1 | grep err   vs   cmd | grep err', 'Errors go to stderr, which doesn\'t enter the pipe unless you merge it.', 'cmd 2>&1 | grep err'], ['sort file | uniq without sort', 'uniq alone only removes ADJACENT duplicates.', 'sort file | uniq']],
  safe: 'Pipes don\'t touch files. (Commands inside the pipeline still might.)',
  r: [['redir', '> sends output to a FILE; | sends it to a COMMAND.'], ['tee', 'Save to a file AND keep piping.'], ['xargs', 'Turn piped lines into command-line ARGUMENTS.']],
  cs: ['<code>a | b</code>: a\'s stdout → b\'s stdin', 'All commands run simultaneously', 'stderr is NOT piped (use <code>2>&1 |</code>)', 'Exit code = last command (pipefail to change)', 'Classic: <code>sort | uniq -c | sort -rn</code>'],
  q: [['What does | connect?', ['stderr to a file', 'stdout of one command to stdin of the next', 'two files', 'two terminals'], 1, 'Pipes connect fd 1 → fd 0.'], ['Do the commands in a pipeline run one after another?', ['Yes, strictly in order', 'No, they run at the same time', 'Only on multi-core CPUs', 'Only with &'], 1, 'They run concurrently, synchronised by the pipe buffer.'], ['Do error messages go through the pipe?', ['Yes', 'No, stderr goes to the terminal', 'Only with -v', 'Only for grep'], 1, 'Only stdout is piped unless you add 2>&1.']],
  c: { t: 'Count how many lines of <code>fruits.txt</code> contain “apple” — using a pipe.', try: 'grep apple fruits.txt | wc -l', chk: ctx => { const p = ctx.pipes[ctx.pipes.length - 1]; return p && p.cmds.length >= 2 && outOf(ctx).trim() === '3'; } },
});
lesson('redir', {
  n: '> (redirect)', k: '>', s: 'Sends a command\'s output into a file instead of the screen — replacing the file\'s content.',
  an: 'Pointing a hose into a bucket instead of onto the lawn — but first the bucket is tipped out. Whatever was in it before is gone.',
  nm: 'The <b>&gt;</b> arrow points where the output goes.',
  sx: 'COMMAND > FILE   |   COMMAND 2> FILE   |   COMMAND > FILE 2>&1', p: [['COMMAND', 'cmd', 'Any command that writes to stdout.', true], ['>', 'opt', 'Redirect stdout (fd 1); truncate the file first. 2> redirects stderr (fd 2).', true], ['FILE', 'arg', 'Created if missing, EMPTIED if it exists.', true]],
  ex1: 'ls > listing.txt',
  sc: [['openat', 'the SHELL opens the file before starting the command: create if missing, truncate to 0 bytes', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "listing.txt", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_TRUNC, 0666) = 3'], ['fork', 'start the child process', 'clone(…) = 2205'], ['dup2', 'in the child, make fd 1 point at the file', 'dup2(3, 1) = 1'], ['execve', 'run ls — it writes to fd 1 without knowing it\'s a file', 'execve("/usr/bin/ls", ["ls"], …) = 0'], ['write', 'ls output lands in the file', 'write(1, "backup\\nbin\\n…", 96) = 96']],
  ch: 'The target file is created or completely overwritten.', out: 'Nothing on screen (unless errors: stderr is still the terminal).', ec: 'The command\'s exit code; 1 if the shell couldn\'t open the file (permission denied, directory missing).',
  f: [['>', '', 'stdout → file (overwrite).', 'ls > out.txt', true], ['2>', '', 'stderr → file.', 'find / -name x 2> errors.txt', true], ['2>&1', '', 'stderr → same place as stdout (put it AFTER > file).', 'cmd > all.log 2>&1', true], ['&>', '', 'stdout + stderr → file (bash).', 'cmd &> all.log', true], ['> /dev/null', '', 'Throw output away.', 'cmd > /dev/null 2>&1', true], ['>|', '', 'Overwrite even when noclobber is set.', 'echo x >| f', false], ['set -o noclobber', '', 'Make > refuse to overwrite existing files.', 'set -o noclobber', false]],
  e: [['ls > listing.txt', '', 'Save a directory listing.'], ['echo "first line" > new.txt; cat new.txt', 'first line', 'Create a file with content.'], ['date > now.txt', '', 'Each run REPLACES the file.'], ['ls nope 2> err.txt; cat err.txt', "ls: cannot access 'nope': No such file or directory", 'Capture only errors.'], ['find / -name "*.conf" 2>/dev/null | head -3', '', 'Hide permission-denied noise.'], ['ls docs nope > all.txt 2>&1; cat all.txt', '', 'Both streams into one file.']],
  u: `<p><b>The shell does the redirection, not the command.</b> Before the program starts, the shell opens the file and makes fd 1 point at it (dup2). The program just writes to fd 1 as usual.</p><p>The file is truncated <b>immediately</b> — before the command runs. So <code>sort notes.txt > notes.txt</code> gives an EMPTY file: by the time sort reads it, it has already been emptied. Try it in the terminal!</p><p><b>Order matters:</b> <code>cmd > f 2>&1</code> = both into f. <code>cmd 2>&1 > f</code> = stderr to the terminal (where stdout pointed at that moment), stdout to f.</p><p><code>/dev/null</code> is a special file that discards everything written to it.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>bash processes redirections left to right on the child's fd table: <code>open()</code> then <code>dup2(newfd, 1)</code> and <code>close(newfd)</code>. <code>2>&1</code> is just <code>dup2(1, 2)</code> — it copies whatever fd 1 points to <i>at that moment</i>. With <code>set -o noclobber</code>, bash opens with <code>O_EXCL</code> for existing regular files. Redirections on builtins are done in the shell itself and undone afterwards.</p>`,
  mi: [['sort data.csv > data.csv', 'The file is emptied before sort reads it → data lost.', 'sort data.csv > sorted.csv && mv sorted.csv data.csv   # or sort -o data.csv data.csv'], ['cmd 2>&1 > file', 'stderr still goes to the screen.', 'cmd > file 2>&1'], ['sudo echo x > /etc/f', 'Permission denied: YOUR shell opens the file.', 'echo x | sudo tee /etc/f'], ['echo log >> file  vs  echo log > file', '> wipes previous log entries.', 'Use >> to append']],
  w: '<b>&gt; empties the target file instantly</b>, even if the command then fails or produces nothing — there is no confirmation and no undo. Use <code>&gt;&gt;</code> to append, enable <code>set -o noclobber</code> in ~/.bashrc to protect existing files, and never redirect into a file you\'re reading in the same command.',
  r: [['append', '>> adds to the end instead of replacing.'], ['pipe', '| sends output to another command instead of a file.'], ['tee', 'Write to a file AND still see the output.'], ['stdin', '< feeds a file INTO a command.']],
  cs: ['<code>cmd > f</code> stdout to file (overwrite!)', '<code>cmd 2> f</code> errors to file', '<code>cmd > f 2>&1</code> or <code>&> f</code> both', '<code>> /dev/null</code> discard', 'File is emptied BEFORE the command runs'],
  q: [['What happens to existing content with > ?', ['Appended', 'Replaced (file emptied first)', 'Kept and new content discarded', 'Asked for confirmation'], 1, 'O_TRUNC empties it.'], ['Which captures only error messages?', ['1>', '2>', '&>', '>>'], 1, 'fd 2 is stderr.'], ['Result of sort f > f?', ['f is sorted', 'f is empty', 'Error', 'f is duplicated'], 1, 'The shell truncates f before sort reads it.']],
  c: { t: 'Save the output of <code>ls</code> into a file called <code>listing.txt</code>.', try: 'ls > listing.txt', chk: ctx => { const r = HP('listing.txt'); return r.n && (r.n.data || '').includes('docs'); } },
});
lesson('append', {
  n: '>> (append)', k: '>>', s: 'Adds a command\'s output to the END of a file, keeping what was already there.',
  an: 'Writing a new entry at the bottom of a diary page — the earlier entries stay.',
  nm: 'Two arrows <b>&gt;&gt;</b>: “add more”.',
  sx: 'COMMAND >> FILE   |   COMMAND 2>> FILE', p: [['COMMAND', 'cmd', 'Any command.', true], ['>>', 'opt', 'Redirect stdout in append mode (2>> for stderr).', true], ['FILE', 'arg', 'Created if missing; existing content kept.', true]],
  ex1: 'echo "learn more" >> notes.txt',
  sc: [['openat', 'the shell opens the file with O_APPEND (no O_TRUNC!)', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", O_WRONLY|O_CREAT|O_APPEND, 0666) = 3'], ['dup2', 'fd 1 → the file', 'dup2(3, 1) = 1'], ['write', 'each write is placed at the current end of the file, atomically', 'write(1, "learn more\\n", 11) = 11']],
  ch: 'The file grows; earlier content untouched.', out: 'Nothing on screen.', ec: 'The command\'s exit code.',
  f: [['>>', '', 'Append stdout.', 'date >> log.txt', true], ['2>>', '', 'Append stderr.', 'cmd 2>> errors.log', true], ['>> f 2>&1', '', 'Append both.', 'cmd >> all.log 2>&1', true], ['&>>', '', 'Append both (bash).', 'cmd &>> all.log', false]],
  e: [['echo "learn more" >> notes.txt', '', 'Add a line at the end.'], ['date >> log.txt; date >> log.txt; cat log.txt', '', 'Each run adds a line.'], ['tail -n 2 notes.txt', '', 'Check the end of the file.'], ['ls nope 2>> errors.log', '', 'Collect errors over time.'], ['echo "alias gs=\'git status\'" >> ~/.bashrc', '', 'The classic way to add config lines.']],
  u: `<p>The difference between &gt; and &gt;&gt; is a single open flag: <b>O_TRUNC</b> (empty the file) vs <b>O_APPEND</b> (always write at the end). With O_APPEND the kernel moves the write position to the end <i>atomically</i> before each write, so several processes can append to the same log without overwriting each other's lines.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>For O_APPEND files, <code>generic_file_write_iter</code> sets <code>iocb->ki_pos = i_size_read(inode)</code> under the inode lock for every write, which is why concurrent appends don't clobber each other (on local filesystems — not guaranteed on NFS). Lines longer than PIPE_BUF from different writers can still interleave.</p>`,
  mi: [['echo x > notes.txt (meant to add)', 'Everything else in notes.txt is gone.', 'echo x >> notes.txt'], ['echo "line" >> file (no newline at end of file)', 'If the file didn\'t end with \\n, the new text glues onto the last line.', 'printf "\\nline\\n" >> file'], ['cat file >> file', 'Infinite growth risk — cat refuses: “input file is output file”.', 'cat file > tmp && cat tmp >> file']],
  safe: '>> never deletes existing content — the safe choice when in doubt. (Still mind runaway logs filling the disk.)',
  r: [['redir', '> replaces; >> appends.'], ['tee', 'tee -a appends AND shows output.'], ['cat', 'cat a >> b appends one file to another.']],
  cs: ['<code>cmd >> f</code> append stdout', '<code>2>></code> append stderr', 'Existing content is kept', 'Uses O_APPEND: atomic end-of-file writes', 'In doubt between > and >>, pick >>'],
  q: [['You run echo a > f; echo b >> f. What is in f?', ['a', 'b', 'a then b', 'b then a'], 2, '> creates "a", >> adds "b".'], ['Which open flag does >> use?', ['O_TRUNC', 'O_APPEND', 'O_EXCL', 'O_RDONLY'], 1, 'O_APPEND = always write at the end.'], ['How do you append errors only?', ['2>>', '>>2', '&>', '2>'], 0, '2>> appends stderr.']],
  c: { t: 'Add the line <code>learn more</code> to the END of a notes file (notes.txt or notes-old.txt) without losing its content.', try: 'echo "learn more" >> notes.txt', chk: ctx => ['notes.txt', 'notes-old.txt'].some(f => { const r = HP(f); return r.n && /learn more\n?$/.test(r.n.data || '') && (r.n.data || '').includes('Linux notes'); }) },
});
lesson('stdin', {
  n: '< (input)', k: '<', s: 'Feeds the contents of a file into a command\'s standard input.',
  an: 'Instead of dictating a letter to a typist word by word, you hand them the written page to read from.',
  nm: 'The <b>&lt;</b> arrow points INTO the command.',
  sx: 'COMMAND < FILE', p: [['COMMAND', 'cmd', 'A command that reads stdin.', true], ['<', 'opt', 'Redirect stdin (fd 0).', true], ['FILE', 'arg', 'Must exist and be readable.', true]],
  ex1: 'wc -w < notes.txt',
  sc: [['openat', 'the shell opens the file read-only', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "notes.txt", O_RDONLY) = 3'], ['dup2', 'fd 0 (stdin) now reads from the file', 'dup2(3, 0) = 0'], ['execve', 'wc starts; it reads fd 0 and never learns the file name', 'execve("/usr/bin/wc", ["wc", "-w"], …) = 0'], ['read', 'wc reads its input', 'read(0, "Linux notes\\n…", 16384) = 104']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Whatever the command prints. Note: no file name appears in wc\'s output, because wc only saw stdin.', ec: 'The command\'s; 1 if the file can\'t be opened.',
  f: [['<', '', 'File → stdin.', 'sort < fruits.txt', true], ['<<', '', 'Here-document: inline lines until a marker.', 'cat << EOF … EOF', false], ['<<<', '', 'Here-string: a single string as stdin.', 'wc -w <<< "a b c"', false], ['< f > g', '', 'Combine input and output redirection.', 'sort < a > b', true]],
  e: [['wc -w < notes.txt', '20', 'Word count, no file name printed.'], ['wc -w notes.txt', '20 notes.txt', 'Compare: as an argument, wc knows the name.'], ['sort < fruits.txt', 'apple\napple…', 'Sort reading stdin.'], ['tr a-z A-Z < fruits.txt', 'BANANA…', 'tr ONLY reads stdin, so < is the natural way.'], ['sort < fruits.txt > sorted.txt', '', 'Input and output redirected at once.']],
  u: `<p>Many commands accept a file name OR read stdin when no name is given (cat, sort, wc, grep…). A few, like <code>tr</code>, only read stdin. <code>cmd &lt; file</code> is equivalent to <code>cat file | cmd</code> but needs no extra process and no pipe.</p><p>Because the shell opens the file, a missing file is reported by bash (“bash: x: No such file or directory”), not by the command.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>Here-documents (<code>&lt;&lt;EOF</code>) are implemented by bash writing the text to a temporary file or pipe and dup2'ing it onto fd 0; with a quoted marker (<code>&lt;&lt;'EOF'</code>) no expansion happens. Here-strings (<code>&lt;&lt;&lt;</code>) do the same with one word plus a newline.</p>`,
  mi: [['cat < file1 file2', 'cat reads the arguments and ignores stdin.', 'cat file1 file2'], ['tr a-z A-Z fruits.txt', 'tr: extra operand — tr takes no file argument.', 'tr a-z A-Z < fruits.txt'], ['sort < file > file', 'The output file is truncated first → empty input.', 'sort -o file file']],
  safe: '< only reads the file.',
  r: [['redir', '> is the output direction.'], ['pipe', 'cmd1 | cmd2 feeds another COMMAND instead of a file.'], ['cat', 'cat f | cmd is the longer equivalent.']],
  cs: ['<code>cmd < f</code> file → stdin', 'The command never sees the file name', '<code>tr</code> needs it: <code>tr a-z A-Z < f</code>', 'Combine: <code>sort < in > out</code>', '<code>&lt;&lt;EOF</code> here-doc, <code>&lt;&lt;&lt;</code> here-string'],
  q: [['Why does wc -l < f not print the file name?', ['Bug', 'wc only sees stdin, not the name', 'Because of -l', 'It does'], 1, 'The shell opened the file.'], ['Which command ONLY reads stdin?', ['cat', 'sort', 'tr', 'grep'], 2, 'tr takes no file arguments.'], ['Who reports “No such file” for cmd < missing?', ['cmd', 'the shell (bash)', 'the kernel log', 'nobody'], 1, 'bash opens the file before starting cmd.']],
  c: { t: 'Count the words in notes.txt (or notes-old.txt) by feeding it to <code>wc</code> with <code>&lt;</code>.', try: 'wc -w < notes.txt', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'wc') && ctx.events.some(e => e.type === 'redir' && e.op === '<') && ctx.code === 0 },
});
lesson('grep', {
  n: 'grep', s: 'Searches text for lines matching a pattern and prints those lines.',
  an: 'A highlighter pen run over a long document: only the lines containing your word are kept.',
  nm: 'From the old ed editor command <b>g/re/p</b>: <b>g</b>lobally search a <b>r</b>egular <b>e</b>xpression and <b>p</b>rint.',
  sx: 'grep [OPTIONS] PATTERN [FILE]...', p: [['grep', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-i ignore case, -v invert, -n line numbers, -c count, -r recursive…', false], ['PATTERN', 'arg', 'Text or regular expression. Quote it if it has spaces or special characters.', true], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Where to search; none = stdin (pipes).', false]],
  ex1: 'grep -n ERROR logs/app.log',
  sc: [['openat', 'opens each file', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "logs/app.log", O_RDONLY|O_NOCTTY) = 3'], ['read', 'reads big chunks; the regex engine scans the buffer line by line', 'read(3, "2026-11-18 09:00:12 INFO …", 98304) = 2290'], ['write', 'prints matching lines (colouring the match in a terminal)', 'write(1, "5:2026-11-18 09:04:12 ERROR database…", 600) = 600'], ['close', 'next file', 'close(3) = 0']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Matching lines (with -n numbers, with several files prefixed by file name).', ec: '<b>0 = at least one match, 1 = no match, 2 = error.</b> Useful in scripts: <code>if grep -q x f; then …</code>',
  f: [['-i', '--ignore-case', 'Case-insensitive.', 'grep -i error app.log', true], ['-v', '--invert-match', 'Lines that do NOT match.', 'grep -v INFO app.log', true], ['-n', '--line-number', 'Show line numbers.', 'grep -n TODO notes.txt', true], ['-c', '--count', 'Count matching lines.', 'grep -c apple fruits.txt', true], ['-r', '--recursive', 'Search all files under a directory.', 'grep -r TODO .', true], ['-l', '--files-with-matches', 'Only print file names.', 'grep -rl nginx /etc', false], ['-w', '--word-regexp', 'Match whole words only.', 'grep -w app f', false], ['-o', '--only-matching', 'Print only the matched part.', 'grep -o "[0-9]\\+" f', false], ['-E', '--extended-regexp', 'Extended regex: + ? | () without backslashes.', 'grep -E "WARN|ERROR" f', true], ['-F', '--fixed-strings', 'Literal text, no regex.', 'grep -F "a.b" f', false], ['-A/-B/-C N', '', 'Show N lines after/before/around matches.', 'grep -C 2 ERROR f', false], ['-q', '--quiet', 'No output; just the exit code.', 'grep -q x f && echo found', false]],
  e: [['grep apple fruits.txt', 'apple\napple\napple', 'Lines containing apple.'], ['grep -n ERROR logs/app.log', '5:… ERROR database timeout…', 'With line numbers.'], ['grep -c apple fruits.txt', '3', 'Count only.'], ['grep -v INFO logs/app.log | head -3', '', 'Everything except INFO lines.'], ['grep -E "WARN|ERROR" logs/app.log | wc -l', '', 'Two patterns at once.'], ['grep -i "^l" notes.txt', 'Linux notes', '^ = start of line, case-insensitive.'], ['ps aux | grep nginx', '', 'Filter another command\'s output.']],
  u: `<p>grep reads input <b>line by line</b> and tests each line against a <b>regular expression</b>: <code>.</code> any character, <code>*</code> repeat, <code>^</code> start of line, <code>$</code> end, <code>[0-9]</code> a digit, <code>\\b</code> word boundary. Plain words just match themselves.</p><p>Quote patterns: <code>grep "two words" f</code>, and protect special characters from the shell (<code>grep '\\$HOME' f</code>). <b>Exit codes</b> make grep a test in scripts: <code>grep -q pattern f &amp;&amp; echo yes</code>.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>GNU grep compiles the pattern to a DFA and uses Boyer–Moore for fixed strings, searching whole buffers rather than splitting lines first — that's why it can be faster than reading line-by-line. With <code>-P</code> it uses PCRE (lookaheads etc.). For binary files it prints “Binary file matches” unless <code>-a</code>. <code>LC_ALL=C grep</code> is much faster for ASCII data.</p>`,
  mi: [['grep error app.log (log says ERROR)', 'No match — grep is case-sensitive.', 'grep -i error app.log'], ['grep *.txt notes', 'The shell expands *.txt into file names → grep treats the first one as the pattern!', 'grep "pattern" *.txt'], ['grep "a|b" f', 'In basic regex | is literal.', 'grep -E "a|b" f'], ['ps aux | grep nginx (shows the grep itself)', 'grep\'s own command line contains "nginx".', 'pgrep -a nginx   # or grep [n]ginx']],
  safe: 'grep is read-only.',
  r: [['grep-r', 'Recursive search through folders.'], ['awk', 'awk can filter AND pick columns / compute.'], ['sed', 'sed can filter AND replace.'], ['find', 'find searches file NAMES/attributes; grep searches CONTENT.']],
  cs: ['<code>grep word file</code>', '<code>-i</code> ignore case · <code>-v</code> invert · <code>-n</code> numbers', '<code>-c</code> count · <code>-r</code> recursive · <code>-l</code> names only', '<code>-E "a|b"</code> extended regex', 'Exit 0 = found, 1 = not found'],
  q: [['Which flag prints lines that do NOT match?', ['-n', '-v', '-x', '-o'], 1, '-v inverts.'], ['grep -c apple fruits.txt prints…', ['the lines', 'the number of matching lines', 'apple\'s position', 'the file name'], 1, '-c counts matching lines.'], ['What exit code means “no match”?', ['0', '1', '2', '127'], 1, '0 match, 1 none, 2 error.']],
  c: { t: 'Find all <b>ERROR</b> lines in <code>logs/app.log</code> (and only those).', try: 'grep ERROR logs/app.log', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'grep') && outOf(ctx).includes('ERROR') && !outOf(ctx).includes('INFO') },
});
lesson('wc', {
  n: 'wc', s: 'Counts lines, words and bytes (characters) in text.',
  an: 'A teacher\'s word-counter for essays: how many lines, how many words, how many letters.',
  nm: '<b>w</b>ord <b>c</b>ount.',
  sx: 'wc [OPTIONS] [FILE]...', p: [['wc', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-l lines, -w words, -c bytes, -m characters.', false], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Files or stdin.', false]],
  ex1: 'wc -l data.csv',
  sc: [['openat', 'opens the file', 'openat(AT_FDCWD, "data.csv", O_RDONLY) = 3'], ['read', 'reads chunks and counts newline characters / word boundaries', 'read(3, "name,dept,salary,city\\n…", 16384) = 138'], ['write', 'prints the counts', 'write(1, "6 data.csv\\n", 11) = 11']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'Columns: lines, words, bytes, file name (only the ones you asked for). From stdin, no name.', ec: '0 or 1 on error.',
  f: [['-l', '--lines', 'Count newline characters.', 'wc -l f', true], ['-w', '--words', 'Count whitespace-separated words.', 'wc -w f', true], ['-c', '--bytes', 'Count bytes.', 'wc -c f', true], ['-m', '--chars', 'Count characters (differs from bytes for UTF-8).', 'wc -m f', false], ['-L', '--max-line-length', 'Length of the longest line.', 'wc -L f', false]],
  e: [['wc notes.txt', '  5  20 104 notes.txt', 'lines words bytes name.'], ['wc -l data.csv', '6 data.csv', 'Lines only (header + 5 rows).'], ['ls | wc -l', '', 'How many entries in the directory.'], ['grep -c ERROR logs/app.log', '', 'Alternative to grep | wc -l.'], ['wc -l *.txt', '', 'Several files plus a total.']],
  u: `<p><code>wc -l</code> counts <b>newline characters</b>, so a last line without a trailing newline isn't counted. Words are sequences separated by spaces/tabs/newlines. Bytes ≠ characters for non-ASCII text: “é” is 2 bytes in UTF-8.</p><p>wc is the standard “how many?” tool at the end of a pipeline.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>coreutils wc has fast paths: for -c on regular files it just uses <code>st_size</code> (fstat) without reading; for -l it uses <code>memchr</code>/AVX2 to count \\n in large buffers. For -m it decodes multibyte sequences according to the locale (LC_CTYPE).</p>`,
  mi: [['wc -l file (file has no final newline)', 'Shows one line fewer than you expect.', 'Add a newline at the end, or use grep -c ""'], ['ls -l | wc -l', 'Includes the “total” line → off by one.', 'ls | wc -l'], ['wc file | cut … to get the number', 'The file name is in the output.', 'wc -l < file']],
  safe: 'Read-only.',
  r: [['grep', 'grep -c counts matching lines directly.'], ['uniq', 'uniq -c counts repeats.'], ['du', 'du measures disk usage, not text.']],
  cs: ['<code>wc f</code> lines words bytes', '<code>wc -l</code> lines · <code>-w</code> words · <code>-c</code> bytes', '<code>cmd | wc -l</code> count output lines', '<code>wc -l < f</code> number only', 'Counts newline characters'],
  q: [['Which flag counts lines?', ['-n', '-l', '-c', '-w'], 1, '-l = lines.'], ['What does ls | wc -l count?', ['bytes in ls', 'entries in the directory', 'hidden files', 'subdirectories only'], 1, 'Piped ls prints one name per line.'], ['How to get just the number without the file name?', ['wc -n f', 'wc -l < f', 'wc -q f', 'wc f -l'], 1, 'Via stdin, wc has no name to print.']],
  c: { t: 'Find out how many lines <code>data.csv</code> has.', try: 'wc -l data.csv', chk: ctx => okRun(ctx, 'wc') && /\b6\b/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
lesson('sort', {
  n: 'sort', s: 'Sorts lines of text — alphabetically, numerically, by column, reversed…',
  an: 'Arranging a stack of index cards in order — by the first letter, or by the number written in the corner.',
  nm: '<b>sort</b>.',
  sx: 'sort [OPTIONS] [FILE]...', p: [['sort', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-n numeric, -r reverse, -k field, -t separator, -u unique.', false], ['[FILE]...', 'arg', 'Files or stdin.', false]],
  ex1: 'sort -t, -k3 -n data.csv',
  sc: [['openat + read', 'reads the WHOLE input into memory (it must see the last line before printing the first)', 'read(3, "name,dept,salary,city\\n…", 131072) = 138'], ['(sorting)', 'compares lines (merge sort); for huge inputs writes sorted chunks to temp files in /tmp and merges them', ''], ['write', 'prints the sorted lines', 'write(1, "name,dept,salary,city\\ndave,ops,58000…", 138) = 138']],
  ch: 'Nothing (unless -o FILE).', out: 'The same lines in a new order.', ec: '0, or 2 on error.',
  f: [['-n', '--numeric-sort', 'Compare as numbers (so 9 < 10).', 'sort -n nums', true], ['-r', '--reverse', 'Reverse the order.', 'sort -rn', true], ['-k N', '--key', 'Sort by field N.', 'sort -k2 f', true], ['-t C', '--field-separator', 'Field separator (default: blanks).', 'sort -t, -k3 -n f.csv', true], ['-u', '--unique', 'Drop duplicate lines.', 'sort -u f', true], ['-h', '--human-numeric-sort', 'Sort 2K, 1M, 3G correctly.', 'du -h | sort -h', false], ['-f', '--ignore-case', 'Fold case.', 'sort -f f', false], ['-o FILE', '--output', 'Write to FILE (safe even if it\'s the input).', 'sort -o f f', false], ['-R', '--random-sort', 'Shuffle.', 'sort -R f', false]],
  e: [['sort fruits.txt', 'apple\napple\napple\nbanana\nbanana\ncherry\ndate', 'Alphabetical.'], ['sort -r fruits.txt', '', 'Reverse.'], ['sort -u fruits.txt', 'apple\nbanana\ncherry\ndate', 'Sorted and de-duplicated.'], ['sort -t, -k3 -n data.csv', '', 'By salary (3rd comma-separated field), numerically.'], ['sort -t, -k3 -nr data.csv | head -3', '', 'Top 3 salaries.'], ['du -sh * | sort -h', '', 'Smallest to largest folders.']],
  u: `<p>Default sorting is <b>text order</b>, character by character: “10” comes before “9” because “1” &lt; “9”. Use <b>-n</b> for numbers. Fields are numbered from 1; <code>-k3</code> means “from field 3 to the end of the line” — use <code>-k3,3</code> to use ONLY field 3.</p><p>sort must read all its input before it can output anything, so in a pipeline it is a “blocking” step.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>GNU sort uses a parallel merge sort; when input exceeds the buffer (<code>-S</code>, default ~ a fraction of RAM) it spills sorted runs to <code>$TMPDIR</code> and does an n-way merge — it can sort files larger than memory. Ordering depends on the locale's collation (LC_COLLATE); <code>LC_ALL=C sort</code> gives byte order and is much faster.</p>`,
  mi: [['sort numbers.txt', '10 sorts before 9 (text order).', 'sort -n numbers.txt'], ['sort -k2 file (meaning only column 2)', 'Ties are broken using the rest of the line.', 'sort -k2,2 file'], ['sort f > f', 'f becomes empty.', 'sort -o f f']],
  safe: 'sort only writes to stdout (or -o FILE).',
  r: [['uniq', 'uniq needs sorted input to remove all duplicates.'], ['head', 'Top N after sorting.'], ['cut', 'Pick columns before or after sorting.']],
  cs: ['<code>sort f</code> alphabetical', '<code>-n</code> numeric · <code>-r</code> reverse', '<code>-t, -k3</code> by 3rd comma field', '<code>-u</code> unique · <code>-h</code> human sizes', '<code>sort -o f f</code> sort in place safely'],
  q: [['Why is 10 before 9 with plain sort?', ['Bug', 'Text comparison: "1" < "9"', 'It sorts by length', 'Random'], 1, 'Use -n for numbers.'], ['How to sort a CSV by its 2nd column?', ['sort -2 f', 'sort -t, -k2 f', 'sort -c2 f', 'sort -f2 f'], 1, '-t sets the separator, -k the field.'], ['Which removes duplicates while sorting?', ['-d', '-u', '-x', '-r'], 1, '-u unique.']],
  c: { t: 'Sort <code>data.csv</code> <b>numerically by salary</b> (3rd comma-separated field). Hint: <code>-t, -k3 -n</code>', try: 'sort -t, -k3 -n data.csv', chk: ctx => { const o = outOf(ctx); return okRun(ctx, 'sort') && /-\w*n/.test(ctx.line) && o.indexOf('dave') >= 0 && o.indexOf('dave') < o.indexOf('carol'); } },
});
lesson('uniq', {
  n: 'uniq', s: 'Removes (or counts) duplicate lines that are next to each other.',
  an: 'A cashier scanning items on the belt: identical items in a row are rung up as “3 × apple”. If the apples are scattered across the belt, they get counted separately — so you sort the belt first.',
  nm: '<b>uniq</b>ue.',
  sx: 'uniq [OPTIONS] [INPUT [OUTPUT]]', p: [['uniq', 'cmd', 'The command.', true], ['[OPTIONS]', 'opt', '-c count, -d only duplicates, -u only unique, -i ignore case.', false], ['[INPUT [OUTPUT]]', 'arg', 'Input file (or stdin) and optional output file.', false]],
  ex1: 'sort fruits.txt | uniq -c',
  sc: [['read', 'reads stdin line by line', 'read(0, "apple\\napple\\napple\\nbanana…", 4096) = 45'], ['(compare)', 'keeps only the previous line in memory and compares it with the current one', ''], ['write', 'prints each group once (with -c: the count first)', 'write(1, "      3 apple\\n…", 52) = 52']],
  ch: 'Nothing.', out: 'One line per group of identical ADJACENT lines.', ec: '0.',
  f: [['-c', '--count', 'Prefix lines with the number of occurrences.', 'sort f | uniq -c', true], ['-d', '--repeated', 'Only print lines that are repeated.', 'sort f | uniq -d', true], ['-u', '--unique', 'Only print lines that appear once.', 'sort f | uniq -u', true], ['-i', '--ignore-case', 'Case-insensitive comparison.', 'uniq -i', false], ['-f N', '--skip-fields', 'Ignore the first N fields when comparing.', 'uniq -f1', false]],
  e: [['uniq fruits.txt', 'banana\napple\ncherry\napple\nbanana\napple\ndate', 'Unsorted: nothing is adjacent, so nothing is removed!'], ['sort fruits.txt | uniq', 'apple\nbanana\ncherry\ndate', 'Sort first, then de-duplicate.'], ['sort fruits.txt | uniq -c', '      3 apple\n      2 banana\n      1 cherry\n      1 date', 'Count each.'], ['sort fruits.txt | uniq -c | sort -rn | head -1', '      3 apple', 'Most frequent item — a very common pattern.'], ['sort fruits.txt | uniq -d', 'apple\nbanana', 'Only items that occur more than once.']],
  u: `<p>uniq only remembers the <b>previous line</b>, so it uses almost no memory and works on endless streams — but it can only collapse duplicates that are <b>adjacent</b>. That's why it's almost always used after <code>sort</code>. (<code>sort -u</code> does sort + uniq in one step, but can't count.)</p><p>The pattern <code>sort | uniq -c | sort -rn</code> answers “what are the most common X?” — top IP addresses in a log, most used commands in your history, etc.</p>`,
  adv: `<p>uniq is O(n) time, O(1) memory (two line buffers). Comparison respects LC_COLLATE unless you use LC_ALL=C. For “unique without sorting, keeping first occurrence order” use awk: <code>awk '!seen[$0]++' file</code> (O(unique lines) memory).</p>`,
  mi: [['uniq fruits.txt', 'Duplicates remain because they aren\'t adjacent.', 'sort fruits.txt | uniq'], ['sort f | uniq -c | sort', 'Counts sort as text: "10" before "9".', 'sort f | uniq -c | sort -rn'], ['uniq in.txt in.txt', 'Second argument is the OUTPUT file — it overwrites the input!', 'uniq in.txt out.txt']],
  safe: 'Read-only (careful: a second argument is an output file).',
  r: [['sort', 'Sort first; or sort -u to de-duplicate directly.'], ['wc', 'Count lines overall.'], ['awk', 'awk \'!seen[$0]++\' de-duplicates without sorting.']],
  cs: ['Only removes ADJACENT duplicates', '<code>sort f | uniq</code> = unique lines', '<code>uniq -c</code> counts', '<code>sort | uniq -c | sort -rn</code> = top items', '<code>-d</code> repeated only · <code>-u</code> single only'],
  q: [['Why does uniq on unsorted input leave duplicates?', ['Bug', 'It only compares neighbouring lines', 'It needs -a', 'It ignores case'], 1, 'Sort first.'], ['What prints "3 apple"?', ['uniq -n', 'sort | uniq -c', 'wc -l apple', 'grep -c'], 1, 'uniq -c counts groups.'], ['Which shows only lines that occur once?', ['-d', '-u', '-c', '-i'], 1, '-u = unique only.']],
  c: { t: 'Count how many times each fruit appears in <code>fruits.txt</code> (expect “3 apple”).', try: 'sort fruits.txt | uniq -c', chk: ctx => ran(ctx, 'uniq') && /3 apple/.test(outOf(ctx)) },
});
